Ah, it won't because you'll get an error in R -- the error is *not* in assign, but in the evaluation which will return null:

$ (cd examples/; javac -cp ../JRI.jar Simback.java) && sh run Simback
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.NullPointerException
	at Simback.main(Simback.java:14)

The error is at:
        double[] b=a.asDoubleArray();

because a is null from re.eval().

This is the reason:

> k = "i"
> k=k/10
Error in k/10 : non-numeric argument to binary operator

so the error is in your R code.

If you want the error back in Java, try this:

        REXP a=re.eval("try({ k=k/10 }, silent=TRUE)");
        REXP cl = a.getAttribute("class");
        if (cl != null && cl.asString() != null && cl.asString().equals("try-error")) {
            System.err.println("R error: "+a.asString());
            System.exit(1);
        }


$ (cd examples/; javac -cp ../JRI.jar Simback.java) && sh run Simback
R error: Error in k/10 : non-numeric argument to binary operator

It's a bit easier in the high-level org.rosuda.REngine API:

import org.rosuda.REngine.REXP;
import org.rosuda.REngine.REngine;

public class Simback{
    public static void main(String[] args){
	try {
	    final REngine re = REngine.engineForClass("org.rosuda.REngine.JRI.JRIEngine");
	    double[] i={10};
	    re.assign("k","i"); //THIS DOESN'T work when re.assign("k","i"); is used
	    REXP a=re.parseAndEval("try({ k=k/10 }, silent=TRUE)");
	    if (a.inherits("try-error")) {
		System.err.println("R error: "+a.asString());
		System.exit(1);
	    }
	    double[] b=a.asDoubles();
	    System.out.println(b[0]);
	} catch (Throwable e) {
	    System.err.println("Exception: "+e);
	    e.printStackTrace();
	}
	System.exit(0);
    }
}


$ (cd examples/; javac -cp ../JRI.jar:../REngine.jar:../JRIEngine.jar Simback.java) && CLASSPATH=REngine.jar:JRIEngine.jar:JRI.jar:examples sh run Simback
R error: Error in k/10 : non-numeric argument to binary operator


Cheers,
Simon


